  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>What is ping?</h2>
      <p>
        Ping (latency) is the round-trip time for a tiny packet of data to travel from
        your device to a server and back, measured in milliseconds (ms). Think of it as
        the <em>reaction time</em> of your connection: speed is how much water flows
        through the pipe, ping is how long the water takes to arrive.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ping is mostly decided by distance and the route your data takes. A server in
        Karachi will ping at 10–30&nbsp;ms from a Pakistani home; a server in Europe at
        120–180&nbsp;ms; a US server at 200&nbsp;ms or more. No package upgrade shortens
        the physical distance.
      </p>

      <h2>What counts as good ping?</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Ping</th><th>Rating</th><th>Feels like</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Under 20 ms</strong></td><td>Excellent</td><td>Instant — ideal for competitive gaming</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>20 – 50 ms</strong></td><td>Good</td><td>Smooth gaming and calls</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>50 – 100 ms</strong></td><td>Fair</td><td>Fine for calls; gamers start to feel delay</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>100 – 150 ms</strong></td><td>Poor</td><td>Noticeable lag in games, delayed calls</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Over 150 ms</strong></td><td>Bad</td><td>Games feel unresponsive, calls talk over each other</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>What is jitter?</h2>
      <p>
        Jitter is the <em>variation</em> in ping. If your ping is 40&nbsp;ms, then
        42&nbsp;ms, then 39&nbsp;ms, jitter is tiny and everything feels steady. If it
        swings from 30&nbsp;ms to 150&nbsp;ms and back, jitter is high — and that
        inconsistency is what makes voices stutter on calls and characters teleport in
        games, even when the average ping looks fine.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Under 30 ms jitter</strong> — good for calls and gaming.</li>
        <li><strong>30–50 ms</strong> — occasional stutters.</li>
        <li><strong>Over 50 ms</strong> — calls break up, games rubber-band.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why is my ping high?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Server distance.</strong> Playing on European or US servers from Pakistan adds 100+&nbsp;ms by physics alone. Choose Asian / Middle East servers when a game offers them.</li>
        <li><strong>Wi-Fi.</strong> Wireless adds latency and jitter versus a cable. Competitive gamers should always play wired.</li>
        <li><strong>Other traffic.</strong> Someone streaming 4K or uploading files on the same connection spikes everyone's ping. This is called bufferbloat.</li>
        <li><strong>Routing.</strong> Sometimes your ISP routes traffic on a slow path. A VPN can occasionally <em>lower</em> ping by taking a better route — but usually it adds latency, so test with and without.</li>
        <li><strong>Line problems.</strong> On DSL, a noisy line causes errors and retransmissions that show up as jitter. See <a href="/guides/improve-ptcl-speed">how to improve your PTCL speed</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to lower your ping</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Use an Ethernet cable</strong> — the single biggest improvement for most homes.</li>
        <li><strong>Pick the nearest game server</strong> — Asia, Middle East or Singapore servers beat Europe.</li>
        <li><strong>Close background apps</strong> — pause downloads, cloud sync and streams on all devices while gaming.</li>
        <li><strong>Restart your router</strong> — clears congestion and can pick a better route.</li>
        <li><strong>Try a wired DNS change</strong> — 1.1.1.1 or 8.8.8.8 sometimes helps routing slightly.</li>
        <li><strong>Consider fibre</strong> — Flash Fiber typically has lower and steadier ping than DSL.</li>
      </ol>

